## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design crash‑consistent data paths in persistent memory, a common production issue when power failures occur during in‑flight operations.

## Key Concepts

- Durability guarantees for PMEM writes
- Memory ordering: release/acquire, fences, cacheline flushes
- Recovery via redo log and slot sequence counters
- ABA avoidance in a concurrent ring
- Testing with fault injection and crash replay

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo-code: enqueue/dequeue with PMEM flush/fence
// Slot: { data, seq, valid }
void enqueue(T v){
  size_t t = tail.fetch_add(1);
  Slot *s = &ring[t % CAP];
  s->data = v; // write data
  clwb(&s->data, sizeof(T));
  fence(); // ensure data persists
  s->seq = t; // publish with durable tail update (release)
  clwb(&s->seq, sizeof(size_t));
  fence();
  // publish tail already happens via atomic tail update
}

bool dequeue(T *out){
  size_t h0 = head.load();
  Slot *s = &ring[h0 % CAP];
  if (s->seq != h0) return false;
  *out = s->data;
  s->valid = false;
  clwb(&s->valid, sizeof(bool));
  fence();
  head.fetch_add(1);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle power loss during the publish vs data write steps?
- How would you validate recovery correctness across multiple restart scenarios?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical memory-management skills: implementing per-thread caching, lock-free global pools, ABA avoidance, and handling thread cleanup.

## Key Concepts
- **Thread-local caches**: Minimize contention by keeping allocations per-thread
- **Lock-free free-list**: Use CAS (compare-and-swap) for thread-safe global pool access
- **ABA avoidance**: Tagged pointers or epoch-based schemes prevent the ABA problem where a recycled pointer is misinterpreted as valid
- **Burst refills**: Refill local caches in batches to reduce global pool access frequency
- **Cache locality**: Keeping objects per-thread improves CPU cache hit rates
- **Safe cleanup**: Handle releasing per-thread buffers back to the global pool when threads exit

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Block { struct Block *next; char pad[56]; } Block;

typedef struct { _Atomic(Block *) head; } FreeList;

static inline void* pool_alloc(FreeList *g, _Atomic(Block *) *local, int *lcnt) {
    if (*lcnt == 0) {
        Block *batch[32];
        Block *head = atomic_load_explicit(&g->head, memory_order_acquire);
        do { for (int i = 0; i < 31; i++) batch[i] = batch[i]->next; } 
        while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&g->head, &head, batch[31]));
        *lcnt = 31;
        return batch[0];
    }
    return local[--*lcnt];
}

static inline void pool_free(FreeList *g, _Atomic(Block *) *local, int *lcnt, void *ptr) {
    if (*lcnt == 32) {
        Block *head = atomic_load_explicit(&g->head, memory_order_relaxed);
        do { local[31] = head; } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&g->head, &head, local[0]));
        *lcnt = 0;
    }
    local[(*lcnt)++] = ptr;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of lock-free free-lists, memory safety, and practical memory management in low-level code. It also checks defensive programming (double-free) and how to reason about under-concurrency correctness.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free free-list stored inside blocks; head is an atomic pointer; push/pop via CAS loops
- Alignment and pool boundaries; membership checks to detect invalid frees
- Avoids dynamic allocations; upfront memory footprint predictable
- Stress testing under contention and memory ordering effects

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-C-like representation
typedef struct Block {
  struct Block* next;
  char payload[SIZE];
} Block;
Block pool[N];
_A< Block* > head;

Block* allocate() {
  Block* old = atomic_load(&head);
  while (old) {
    Block* next = old->next;
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&head, &old, next))
      return old;
    old = atomic_load(&head);
  }
  return NULL;
}

void free(Block* b) {
  Block* old = atomic_load(&head);
  do {
    b->next = old;
  } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&head, &old, b));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect and prevent ABA in this setup?
- How would you validate correctness with a multithreaded stress test and measure throughput?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of per-thread buffering, cache-line alignment, and atomic merges to minimize contention in high-frequency workloads.

## Key Concepts
- Thread-local buffers to avoid contention
- Cache-line alignment to prevent false sharing
- Atomic merges with memory_order_relaxed for performance

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdalign.h>
#define NBINS 256

typedef struct {
  alignas(64) unsigned long long bins[NBINS];
} thread_hist_t;

unsigned long long global_bins[NBINS];

void add_sample(thread_hist_t* t, unsigned int v) {
  t->bins[v]++;
}

void merge_all_to_global(thread_hist_t* t) {
  for (int i = 0; i < NBINS; ++i)
    __atomic_fetch_add(&global_bins[i], t->bins[i], __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
  // optionally clear t->bins here
}
```

